<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Lithuania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Taryba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, or Taryba, was the body elected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> Conference in September 1917, under German occupation, to represent Lithuania. Chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a>, it signed the Act of Independence on 16 February 1918, and in July 1918 briefly proclaimed a monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-karl-duke-of-urach">Mindaugas II</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat it formed the first governments and gave way to the Constituent Assembly in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania.jpg" alt="Council of Lithuania" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Template:Aleksandra Jurašaitytė · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signatarai.Signatories_of_Lithuania.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the last Partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in 1795, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and the uprisings of 1830 and 1863 failed to free it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied the country in 1915. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> the Germans, preparing for peace talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, allowed the Vilnius Conference to meet from 18 to 23 September 1917, hoping it would ask for closer ties with Germany. Instead it called for an independent Lithuania, with any closer relationship to depend on German recognition, and on 21 September it elected a council of twenty to carry this out. The Germans banned publication of the resolution and censored the council's newspaper, Lietuvos aidas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The first twenty members were aged from 25 to 66 and included eight lawyers, four priests and three agronomists; eight were Christian democrats. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-smetona">Antanas Smetona</a> was elected chairman at the first meeting on 24 September, and the chairman, two vice-chairmen and two secretaries formed the presidium. On 13 July 1918 six members were added, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustinas-voldemaras">Augustinas Voldemaras</a>, while four socialists, among them Steponas Kairys, resigned over the invitation to a king. Six members of the Vilnius Belarusian Council joined in November 1918 and three Jewish activists in December, and by the spring of 1919 the council had almost doubled. There were no women, despite a petition with 20,000 signatures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 11 December 1917 the council agreed to a firm and permanent alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but Germany did not recognise the state or invite it to the talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, including those abroad, disapproved of the declaration. On 16 February 1918, temporarily chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonas-basanavicius">Jonas Basanavičius</a>, the council declared independence again without naming any tie to Germany. Germany recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on 23 March, on the basis of the December declaration. Germany proposed a personal union with the Prussian Hohenzollern dynasty; as an alternative the council invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-karl-duke-of-urach">Wilhelm Karl, Duke of Urach</a>, who was elected king as Mindaugas II on 13 July 1918.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat the council adopted a provisional constitution on 2 November 1918 and annulled the invitation to the king. Voldemaras formed the first government on 11 November, the day of the armistice at Compiègne, and the council began building an army, police and local government. Smetona left the chair in 1919 on becoming the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and was succeeded by Stasys Šilingas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Lithuania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A conference in Vilnius in September 1917 elected a twenty-member <b>Council of Lithuania</b>, the Taryba, chaired by Antanas Smetona, and on 16 February 1918 its members, among them Jonas Basanavičius, signed the Act of Independence, proclaiming an independent democratic state with Vilnius as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The German occupation government permitted a Vilnius Conference in September 1917, which elected a 20-member <b>Council of Lithuania</b> (Taryba).</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>To avoid this, the <b>Council of Lithuania</b> on 11 July 1918 proclaimed a constitutional monarchy and invited Prince Wilhelm of Urach, a Catholic from Wurttemberg, to be king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lithuania-1918">Kingdom of Lithuania (1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, Germany allowed client institutions, including the Lithuanian <b>Taryba</b>, which had declared independence in February 1918, and planned a Duchy of Courland and Semigallia (1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li></ul></section>
